    vec![
        KernelRule {
            rule_id: "fail-stop-deadline-trap-bounded-cross-rank-wait".to_string(),
            gpu_concept: "MECHANISM (the fail-stop enforcement law for waits that cannot fail gracefully; category E -- parallelism/liveness): an in-kernel cross-rank wait inside a CO-RESIDENT launch (cooperative launch, every CTA present -- the exchange IS the grid barrier) cannot return an error: a CTA that gives up and returns early deadlocks the other CTAs still waiting on its record, and a wait without a deadline hangs the launch forever when a peer stops. The deadline's only sound action is a DELIBERATE DEVICE FAULT, and the source shapes it five ways. (a) DEADLINE ON A POLL SUBSET: the spin loop checks the clock only every 1024 misses ('if ((++spins & 1023u) == 0 && GlobalTimer() - start > static_cast<u64>(timeout_ns)) FaultAtNull();' -- identical in AwaitRecord and AwaitBlock; the two_shot variant names the constant 'kDeadlinePolls = 1024; // polls between two reads of the global timer') -- the clock read is the expensive part of the poll loop, so it amortizes behind the cheap tag load. (b) THE FAULT SHAPE: 'A missed wait deadline: store to address 0x0, then trap (the plugin's fail-stop).' -- a volatile store to a fixed faulting address BEFORE the PTX 'trap;', so the host's error state names an illegal-address fault at a recognizable address, and the fn is '__noinline__' ('Kept out of line so the hot path pays no registers for it.'). (c) THE DEADLINE IS DERIVED, NOT TUNED: it comes from the HOST's outer watchdog -- 'DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash waits on another GPU have a deadline below SGLang's watchdog' -- strictly below, so the kernel fault always preempts the external timeout and the failure surfaces as THIS rank's fault, never as a watchdog kill of a healthy-looking step. (d) THE COMPLETENESS LAW: the deadline must reach EVERY reachable wait path -- Kimi K3 'validates SGLang's finite build deadline and compiles it into all six reachable TP8 remote-wait paths; expiry likewise stores through address 0x0 and traps'; a non-finite watchdog value is refused at build, and a 'none' deadline exists only behind an explicit opt-out constant ('Q35_HOP_DEADLINE_NS (0: none) is the consumer's wait deadline, past which it faults at address 0x0 (fail-stop)') -- the completeness census is TESTED (test_deepseek_v41_fail_stop.py, test_kimi_k3_deadline.py). (e) NO PARTIAL OUTPUT: 'A rank that misses it faults at address 0x0, so no token from a failed step is returned' -- the fault precedes any epilogue that would publish the step's result. REGIME: co-resident/megakernel launches whose CTAs exchange through peer-visible memory (Lamport mailboxes, symmetric buffers) -- anywhere a hung rank must fail the whole launch instead of hanging it. ANTI-REGIME: waits that CAN fail gracefully (a host-side poll of a completion event returns -- gpu_60's layer; a two-tier metadata wait with fault CODES returns to a caller that fails the collective -- gpu_118's ladder); single-kernel producer/consumer inside one CTA (no peer to hang on); kernels whose grid is NOT co-resident (a straggler CTA returning early is safe there -- the premise is gone). KINSHIP (do not double-count): faulted-collective-fail-stop-no-numerics-fallback (gpu_118) owns the POLICY (a from-scratch collective stops the engine, never falls back; selector agreement; terminal faults surfaced host-side) -- THIS owns the in-kernel ENFORCEMENT shape that policy's terminal faults ride; relaxed-spin-acquire-confirm-metadata-flag-wait (gpu_118) owns the wait's ORDERING ladder + fault taxonomy with graceful returns -- THIS the deadline's action when a return is impossible (compose: policy -> admission, ladder -> ordering, THIS -> enforcement); deadline-bounded-poll-of-submitted-completion-event (gpu_60) owns the HOST-side deadline poll of a submitted event -- different layer, the host twin; grid-dependency-wait-scoped-to-dependent-operands (gpu_23) owns PDL wait placement, not wait deadlines. DEDUP (T0 re-run at the batch slot, 1051-rule corpus, 3+ vocabularies): grep 'trap;' over the corpus = two PROSE hits (a rule_id 'occupancy-trap', a comment 'no-sync trap') -- ZERO owners of the PTX trap / deliberate device fault; 'globaltimer' = gpu_118's deadline RETURNS kReadyTimeout (graceful), never faults; 'fail.stop|watchdog|address 0x0|fault' = the gpu_118 pair + incidental hits -- none owns the enforcement shape, the poll-subset amortization, or the watchdog-derivation completeness law. The co-residency premise itself ('The exchange is the grid barrier and the cross-rank argmax at once, so the grid must be co-resident (the host launches it cooperatively)') rides THIS rule as its regime statement, never a separate rule. NOT-BENCHABLE-BY-DOMAIN (cross-rank liveness needs 2+ GPUs and a hung peer; the corpus has no CUDA validator): the fixture is an original deterministic state machine whose spin-budget exhaustion reports the SAME fault class the hardware deadline reports (the gpu_118 fixture's spin-budget stand-in precedent, one layer down). P113: the mechanism is the repo's OWN landed decision -- decision content, never an outbound-PR candidate. Source: sgl-project/sgl-nvidia-mega @ c396f2ab6c7e779af326321ef75fb5b6008e6165 (Apache-2.0) -- sgl_nvidia_mega/deepseek_v41/sm103/markov.cu:40-42 (the header law), :193-209 (FaultAtNull + AwaitRecord + the poll subset), :216-228 (AwaitBlock, the block twin), README 'Fail-stop synchronization' (the watchdog derivation + the no-token law); common/sync.py:172-177 (fault_at_null, the CuTe DSL twin, FlashInfer-adapted); qwen3_5/sm103/hop/src/hop.cu header (Q35_HOP_DEADLINE_NS); qwen3_5/sm103/draft/src/mkd_xchg.cu header ('Waits are bounded and trap'); test/kernels/test_deepseek_v41_fail_stop.py + test/unit/test_kimi_k3_deadline.py (the six-path completeness census is TESTED). Quotes verbatim at the pin. Batch 333 / Plan 341 / Research 371.".to_string(),
            ref_shape: "// naive: an unbounded cross-rank wait -- a hung peer hangs the\n// co-resident launch forever; and an early-return escape deadlocks\n// the CTAs still waiting on this rank's record, so there is NO sound\n// graceful exit at all.\n#[derive(Clone, Copy, PartialEq)]\nstruct Rec {\n    tag: u64,\n    key: u64,\n}\n\nconst TAG_MASK: u64 = 0x7FFF;\n\n/// Spin until the peer's record carries our epoch tag. No deadline:\n/// if the peer stopped, this spins forever and the whole co-resident\n/// grid hangs with it. The `give_up` arm is the trap this rule exists\n/// to close -- it looks like an exit, but every other CTA is waiting\n/// on THIS rank's own record, so returning strands them all.\nfn await_record(mailbox: &[Rec], tag: u64, give_up: impl Fn() -> bool) -> Option<u64> {\n    let mut i = 0usize;\n    loop {\n        let r = mailbox[i % mailbox.len()];\n        if r.tag & TAG_MASK == tag {\n            return Some(r.key);\n        }\n        if give_up() {\n            return None; // deadlock: the peers' wait on us never completes\n        }\n        i += 1;\n    }\n}\n".to_string(),
            opt_pattern: "// optimized: deadline on a poll subset, deliberate fault out of line,\n// deadline derived from the host watchdog, reaching every wait path.\nconst SPINS_PER_DEADLINE_CHECK: u32 = 1024;\nconst TAG_MASK: u64 = 0x7FFF;\n\n/// The host's outer watchdog, read once at build; the kernel deadline\n/// is strictly BELOW it, so the fault always preempts the external\n/// timeout. A non-finite host value is refused at build.\nconst HOST_WATCHDOG_NS: u64 = 5_000_000_000;\nconst WAIT_DEADLINE_NS: u64 = HOST_WATCHDOG_NS / 2;\n\n#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]\npub enum RankFault {\n    DeadlineExceeded,\n}\n\n#[derive(Clone, Copy, PartialEq)]\nstruct Rec {\n    tag: u64,\n    key: u64,\n}\n\n/// Out of line: the hot poll path pays no registers for the fault.\n/// On device this is the volatile store to a fixed faulting address\n/// followed by the PTX trap; the fixture models it as the terminal\n/// state the host's error channel observes.\n#[inline(never)]\nfn fault_at_fixed_address() -> RankFault {\n    RankFault::DeadlineExceeded\n}\n\n/// Spin until the record carries `tag`; a miss past the deadline\n/// faults (the fail-stop) instead of hanging or returning early. The\n/// clock read amortizes: it rides the poll subset only.\nfn await_record(rec: &Rec, tag: u64, elapsed_ns: impl Fn() -> u64) -> Result<u64, RankFault> {\n    if rec.tag & TAG_MASK == tag {\n        return Ok(rec.key);\n    }\n    let mut spins: u32 = 0;\n    loop {\n        if rec.tag & TAG_MASK == tag {\n            return Ok(rec.key);\n        }\n        spins = spins.wrapping_add(1);\n        if spins % SPINS_PER_DEADLINE_CHECK == 0 && elapsed_ns() > WAIT_DEADLINE_NS {\n            return Err(fault_at_fixed_address());\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n/// Completeness: EVERY reachable wait path carries the same deadline\n/// (the source compiles its finite build deadline into all six TP8\n/// remote-wait paths); the census walks the set and refuses a path\n/// whose deadline is missing or not strictly below the watchdog.\nconst REACHABLE_WAIT_PATHS: usize = 2;\n\nfn wait_paths_carry_the_deadline(path_deadlines_ns: [Option<u64>; REACHABLE_WAIT_PATHS]) -> bool {\n    path_deadlines_ns\n        .iter()\n        .all(|d| matches!(d, Some(ns) if *ns > 0 && *ns < HOST_WATCHDOG_NS))\n}\n".to_string(),
        },
        KernelRule {
            rule_id: "peer-mailbox-rotation-depth-reader-residency-plus-one".to_string(),
            gpu_concept: "MECHANISM (the cross-launch mailbox recycling law; category E -- parallelism): a PEER-VISIBLE mailbox outlives the launch that writes it -- a peer rank may still be reading the previous launch's records when the next launch starts -- so slots recycle by ROTATION with a never-zero per-writer epoch, never by readiness-counter gating (counters cannot span ranks or launches without the very synchronization the mailbox exists to avoid). Three decision contents. (a) ROTATION DEPTH IS PROVEN READER RESIDENCY + 1, per site: the narrow markov exchange keeps TWO parities ('Staging and mailbox records alternate between two parities so that a launch never overwrites what a peer may still be reading from the previous launch' -- a reader can hold at most one launch behind); the draft exchange keeps THREE slots and proves the bound in-comment ('a slot is rewritten only three calls later, after two more exchanges that each need every rank's packet' -- worst-case reader residency is two exchanges); the hop pipeline keeps three stages ('Three stages rotate, one per hop'); the all-reduce tail keeps three parities where the reader is the previous MoE layer ('the MoE tail reduces in WaveMoeTail and re-arms the previous MoE layer's rows (three parities)') -- deeper residency, deeper rotation. The depth is a STRUCTURAL constant of the exchange, never a tuned knob. (b) THE EPOCH IS THE PER-WRITER LAUNCH COUNT: each CTA counts its own launches and every launch runs every CTA once ('Each CTA counts its own launches (every launch runs every CTA once), so the epoch needs no end-of-launch atomics'); the tag is NEVER ZERO ('Never zero; distinct for the two launches that share a parity slot', the tag form '1u + count % 32767u') so a zero-initialized buffer reads as EMPTY ('Larger is better; 0 means no key'), and the 15-bit wrap stays far beyond any reader's residency. (c) THE RECORD IS ONE ATOMIC-WIDTH WORD {tag, payload}: a producer stores tag and key in ONE relaxed sys-scope store ('st.relaxed.sys.global.u64'), the reader polls the tag field of ONE load ('(value & kTagMask) == tag' then 'value >> kTagBits'); the draft variant puts the epoch in the HIGH half of each 64-bit word ('Every 32-bit payload word travels in a 64-bit word whose high half is the call's epoch ... so arrival is read from the data itself (single-copy-atomic 8-byte accesses)'), stored via 'multimem.st.relaxed.sys.b64' and polled with 'ld.relaxed.sys.u64'; the record format rides gpu_30's packed-poll law -- cite, never re-own -- and the tag-in-payload form is the DATA-PLANE readiness gpu_118's anti-regime names (no acquire-confirm tier needed: an 8-byte aligned relaxed pair is single-copy atomic, so a record either carries the live epoch or it does not). RE-ARM: readers restore the consumed state ('every slot location a round writes is restored to +0 by its reader'; 'a reader polls its own copy until no bf16 -0.0 sentinel is left, sums the ranks in order and writes the sentinel back') -- the reset-before-reuse defense at the rotation layer. REGIME: peer-visible mailbox/state buffers whose lifetime spans launches (co-resident megakernels, persistent exchange buffers over symmetric memory). ANTI-REGIME: slots whose readers are INSIDE the same launch (counter-gated rings own that -- gpu_61); single-consumer queues with shared progress counters; buffers a scheduler-level fence already orders (gpu_138's layer). KINSHIP (do not double-count): ring-overwrite-gated-k-back-readiness (gpu_61) owns distance-K overwrite gating via readiness counters within ONE pipeline -- THIS owns rotation where counters cannot cross the boundary (compose); the scheduler-side reuse fence (gpu_138) owns the HOST-side reuse gate -- THIS the device-side mailbox twin; staggered-segments-two-queue-fences (gpu_82) owns two-queue fence staggering; sentinel-readiness-over-separate-flag + ll-flag-in-data-packed-poll (gpu_30) own the flag FORMATS the records ride; the parity-bank slot indexing (the B272 gpu_118 rider) is the flag-bank face of the same parity instinct. DEDUP (T0 re-run, 3+ vocabularies): grep 'mailbox|lamport' over the corpus = ZERO; 'parity.buffer|epoch.*tag|launch.*counter|cross.launch|slots rotate' = incidental hits only (kv rings, two-queue fences, stage rings); no rule owns rotation depth proven from reader residency + never-zero epochs + cross-launch scope. NOT-BENCHABLE-BY-DOMAIN (needs 2+ GPUs and cross-launch timing; the fixture is an original deterministic state machine: same writer epochs, a slow reader pinned one launch behind, assert no overwrite of a live record and every stale record distinguishable from a live one). P113: the mechanisms are the repo's OWN landed decisions -- decision content, never an outbound-PR candidate. Source: sgl-project/sgl-nvidia-mega @ c396f2ab6c7e779af326321ef75fb5b6008e6165 (Apache-2.0) -- deepseek_v41/sm103/markov.cu:55-62 (the two-parity law + the per-CTA launch counter), :156-165 (the never-zero tag + the '0 means no key' empty convention), :193-210 (StoreRecord/AwaitRecord {tag,key} + the tag poll), qwen3_5/sm103/draft/src/mkd_xchg.cu:15-35 (kSlots = 3 + the residency proof comment + the epoch-high-half + 'Waits are bounded and trap'), qwen3_5/sm103/hop/src/hop.cu:10-24 (three stages + the sentinel re-arm), kimi_k3/cutedsl/common/twoshot.py:1-22 (parity slots + 'every rank gets the same bits' + the three-parity tail), kimi_k3/cutedsl/common/mailbox.py:1-11 (SENTINEL 0x80000000 re-arm), deepseek_v41/sm103/src/two_shot_mhc.cu:6-10 (the +0/-0 empty-marker + the per-row-cluster epoch counter + the bumper cluster flipping unused counters). Quotes verbatim at the pin. Batch 333 / Plan 341 / Research 371.".to_string(),
            ref_shape: "// naive: ONE mailbox slot per peer -- the next launch overwrites it\n// while a slow peer may still be reading the previous launch's record,\n// and a zero-initialized slot is indistinguishable from a written one.\n#[derive(Clone, Copy, PartialEq)]\nstruct Record {\n    epoch: u64,\n    key: u64,\n}\n\nfn mailbox_publish(slot: &mut Record, epoch: u64, key: u64) {\n    // Overwrites whatever a behind reader still holds -- the torn\n    // publish this rule exists to close. Epoch may be zero (a fresh\n    // counter), so a zeroed slot reads as a live epoch-zero record.\n    *slot = Record { epoch, key };\n}\n\nfn mailbox_read(slot: &Record, expected: u64) -> Option<u64> {\n    if slot.epoch == expected {\n        Some(slot.key)\n    } else {\n        None // a zeroed slot and a stale record are the same answer\n    }\n}\n".to_string(),
            opt_pattern: "// optimized: rotation depth = proven reader residency + 1, a never-\n// zero per-writer epoch, and one-word {tag, payload} records where a\n// zero word reads as empty.\nconst ROTATION_DEPTH: usize = 3; // residency 2 + 1: a slot is rewritten\n                                 // only after two full exchanges pass\nconst TAG_MASK: u64 = 0x7FFF;\n\n#[derive(Clone, Copy, PartialEq)]\nstruct Record {\n    word: u64, // high 49 bits payload, low 15 bits epoch tag\n}\n\nimpl Record {\n    const EMPTY: Record = Record { word: 0 }; // zero word = no record ever\n\n    fn tag(&self) -> u64 {\n        self.word & TAG_MASK\n    }\n\n    fn key(&self) -> u64 {\n        self.word >> 15\n    }\n}\n\n/// The per-writer epoch: the launch count, NEVER zero (zero is the\n/// empty convention -- a zero-initialized buffer reads as empty, and\n/// a wrapped tag can never alias it).\nfn epoch_tag(launch_count: u32) -> u64 {\n    1u64 | ((launch_count as u64) & (TAG_MASK - 1))\n}\n\nfn mailbox_publish(slots: &mut [Record; ROTATION_DEPTH], launch_count: u32, key: u64) {\n    let slot = launch_count as usize % ROTATION_DEPTH; // rotate, never gate\n    let tag = epoch_tag(launch_count);\n    slots[slot] = Record {\n        word: (key << 15) | tag, // ONE word: payload and tag move together\n    };\n}\n\nfn mailbox_read(slots: &[Record; ROTATION_DEPTH], launch_count: u32) -> Option<u64> {\n    let slot = launch_count as usize % ROTATION_DEPTH;\n    let r = slots[slot];\n    if r.tag() == epoch_tag(launch_count) {\n        Some(r.key()) // live: this launch's own tag\n    } else {\n        None // EMPTY (zero word) or stale (an older epoch): both refused\n    }\n}\n\n#[cfg(test)]\nmod rotation_gates {\n    use super::*;\n\n    #[test]\n    fn slow_reader_one_launch_behind_still_reads_its_record() {\n        let mut slots = [Record::EMPTY; ROTATION_DEPTH];\n        mailbox_publish(&mut slots, 0, 100);\n        // the writer advances two launches (the proven residency bound)\n        mailbox_publish(&mut slots, 1, 200);\n        mailbox_publish(&mut slots, 2, 300);\n        // launch 3 rewrites slot 0 -- but launch 0's reader has had two\n        // exchanges to finish, so the bound holds BY PROOF: residency 2\n        // + depth 3 means a LIVE record is never rewritten.\n        mailbox_publish(&mut slots, 3, 400);\n        // a reader pinned at launch 2 still reads launch 2's record;\n        // launch 0's record is gone, but its residency has expired.\n        assert_eq!(mailbox_read(&slots, 2), Some(300));\n    }\n\n    #[test]\n    fn zero_initialized_buffer_reads_empty_never_stale_live() {\n        let slots = [Record::EMPTY; ROTATION_DEPTH];\n        assert_eq!(mailbox_read(&slots, 7), None); // tag 8 != 0: refused\n    }\n\n    #[test]\n    fn epoch_is_never_zero_including_launch_zero() {\n        assert_eq!(epoch_tag(0), 1);\n        assert_ne!(epoch_tag(0) & TAG_MASK, 0);\n    }\n}\n".to_string(),
        },
    ]
